.Dd November 24, 2026
.Dt DIG 1
.Os Belka
.Sh NAME
.Nm dig ,
.Nm drill
.Nd ask a DNS server, and show its answer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op @ Ns Ar server
.Op Fl t Ar type
.Op Fl x Ar address
.Op Ar name
.Op Ar type
.Op Cm +short
.Op Cm +norecurse
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
asks for the records of
.Ar type
(A) for
.Ar name
(the root, if none) and prints the reply as BIND's dig does: its
status, and its answer, authority and additional records, names with
their final dots.
.Fl x
asks for an address's PTR record.
.Cm +short
prints only the answers' data.
.Cm +norecurse
asks
.Ar server
without recursion, as djbdns'
.Xr dnsq 1
does; else the query is recursive, as
.Xr dnsqr 1 .
The server is the resolver
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf
names unless
.Ar server ,
an address or a name, is given.
Other options of dig's are accepted and passed over.
Types djbdns does not decode are shown as their number and their bytes
(TXT and AAAA are decoded).
.Pp
.Nm drill
is the same.
Both are a shell script over djbdns, not BIND's dig or ldns' drill.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when a reply came, whatever it said; 1 for bad arguments; 9 when no
server answered.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr host 1 ,
.Xr xdig 1 ,
.Xr dnsq 1 ,
.Xr dnsqr 1
